==== Wave 0: Mechanics

The systems you meet in Wave 0 are the ones every later wave builds on. Learn them in the 172, where they are simple and unhurried.

===== The Primary Instruments

+ *Airspeed indicator* — speed through the air. In the pattern, this is your primary management tool: too slow and the wing stops flying; too fast and you float forever.
+ *Attitude indicator* — the artificial horizon. Pitch and bank are read off it; fly the horizon, trust it over your stomach.
+ *Altimeter* — height above sea level, set to QNH. In the pattern, altitudes are the contract with everyone else in the circuit.
+ *Heading indicator* — compass heading. Turn it, then correct for drift on windy days (crabbing into the wind).
+ *Vertical speed indicator* — climb/descent rate. Lags a moment; use it to confirm the attitude you set, not to chase it.
+ *Turn coordinator* — rate of turn; the standard-rate turn (2 minutes per 360°) is the polite turn in instrument flying later.

===== Powerplant Basics

+ *Throttle* — power. Pull it back and the airplane slows and wants to descend; push it up and it accelerates and climbs.
+ *Mixture* — fuel-air ratio. Rich for takeoff and climb, leaned for cruise. Too lean at full power is a hot engine; too rich at altitude is rough running.
+ *Flaps* — lift and drag on demand. Extending flaps lowers the stall speed and steepens the descent for the approach; retracting them cleans the airplane up for climb.
+ *Fuel management* — tank selector, gauges, and the simple rule: know which tank feeds the engine and how much is in it.

===== Controls & Trim

+ *Pitch, roll, yaw* — elevator (yoke fore/aft), ailerons (yoke left/right), rudder (pedals). Turns are coordinated when the ball in the turn coordinator stays centered — feet chase the ball.
+ *Trim* — the control that holds the airplane so you do not have to. In the 172, trim for the speed you want and the airplane flies itself to a large degree.
+ *Ground handling* — on the ground, the rudder steers (the 172 has a steerable nosewheel via the pedals); brakes stop and turn you tightly. The yoke does almost nothing on the ground.

===== Navigation & Radio

+ *Dead reckoning* — heading + speed + time = where you should be; look outside and confirm.
+ *GPS basics* — the sim's GPS/avionics show position, direct-to navigation, and a flight plan. Wave 0: fly direct and learn the moving map.
+ *Radio stack* — COM (talk), NAV (navaids), transponder (identification). Tune the airport's CTAF, listen to ATIS, and set the transponder code when ATC asks.

===== Weather & Environment

+ *Wind* — the day's wind is the pattern's secret boss: headwind shortens your ground legs, crosswind demands a crab on final and a sideslip at touchdown.
+ *Visibility & ceiling* — VFR weather in Wave 0: clear skies, and if the ceiling looks thin, look for the airport before you leave it.
+ *Live weather* — the sim can pull real-world weather; for training, prefer clear-and-calm until the pattern is automatic.
